        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.248,
            0.832,
            0.269
          ],
          "content": "Using these two points, we can draw the graph of the equation."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.248,
            0.279,
            0.749,
            0.59
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [
            {
              "bbox": [
                0.263,
                0.597,
                0.734,
                0.617
              ],
              "content": "Fig. 13.2: Graph of the linear equation 2x + y = 7"
            }
          ],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_5_image_5.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.114,
            0.626,
            0.882,
            0.668
          ],
          "content": "From the graph, we see that (1, 5), (2, 3), (3, 1), $\\left( { \\frac { 3 } { 2 } } , 4 \\right)$ are also solutions of $2x + y = 7.$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.668,
            0.884,
            0.765
          ],
          "content": "Note that an easy way of identifying two points on a line is to substitute $x = 0$ in the linear equation and obtain the corresponding value of y. This will give us the point where the line intersects the y-axis. Similarly, we can substitute $y = 0$ and obtain the corresponding value of x. This will give us the point where the line intersects the x-axis."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.113,
            0.769,
            0.883,
            0.809
          ],
          "content": "**Example 3:** Find any two solutions for each of the following equations and draw their graphs:"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.168,
            0.813,
            0.679,
            0.834
          ],
          "content": "(i) $4x + 3y = 12$ (ii) 2x + 5y = 0"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.839,
            0.268,
            0.857
          ],
          "content": "**Solution:**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.11,
            0.863,
            0.884,
            0.923
          ],
          "content": "(i) Substituting $x \\quad = \\quad 0$ in the equation, we get $3 y ~ = ~ 1 2 ,$ that is, $y = 4.50 (0,4)$ is a solution of the given equation. Similarly, by substituting $y = 0 ,$ , we get x = 3. Thus, (3, 0) is also a solution."
        }
      ]
    }
